# EXACTLY ONE BRIDGE OWNS AN ERASURE-DIVERGENT RETURN SLOT (#86 D3).
#
# `Src<T>.get(): T?` erases to `get(): object`; `IntSrc : Src<Int>` narrows it to `get(): Int?`. Two synthesizers can
# see that divergence — `CovariantInterfaceReturnBridge`, which bridges a return an override narrowed, and
# `KotlinOverrideSlotBridge`, whose erasure created this one. Both firing puts two private methods with the SAME
# signature and the SAME MethodImpl descriptor on the class, and the emitter binds whichever it matches first; only
# the erasure bridge forwards VIRTUALLY, so a further-derived override is reachable through the base slot only when
# that one wins. The boundary is the divergence itself, and this document is what holds it: no corpus program shows
# the difference, because either bridge makes a two-level hierarchy load and dispatch.
#
# The override keeps its own `Nullable<int32>` return. One erasure bridge states the closed generic slot; the generic
# interface's dedicated existential forwarding bridge states the owner-dependent star slot under its independently
# allocated reserved spelling. They are not two competing bridges for the same MethodImpl descriptor.
+"name":"get","static":false,"override":false,"virtual":true,"vis":"public","params":[],"ret":{"t":"nullable","of":{"t":"fqn","name":"System.Int32"}}
+"name":"dotkt$ovslot$get$0"
+"vis":"private","params":[],"ret":{"t":"fqn","name":"object"}
+"clrInterfaceImpls":[{"owner":{"t":"fqn","name":"Src","args":[{"t":"fqn","name":"System.Int32"}]},"member":"get","arity":0,"params":[],"ret":{"t":"fqn","name":"object"}}]
+"name":"$dotkt_star$get$0","static":false,"override":false,"virtual":true
+"owner":{"t":"fqn","name":"Src$dotkt$star"},"member":"$dotkt_star$get$0"

# And the covariant-return pass does NOT also bridge it.
-dotkt$covar$get$
